speechtext:
I meant our poultry market;
I may have made a slip there. I was pointing out that the poultrymen of British Columbia are questioning whether a continuation of this legislation will give them the security that they need. They feel that this type of legislation leaves everything in the hands of the Minister of Agriculture. They have repeatedly asked that a marketing board be set up upon which those closely in touch with the producer side of things would be largely represented, instead of leaving it all in the hands of some official who is acting as selling agent but who perhaps would not give the same consideration to the maintenance of markets as would be given by those actively engaged in the production of poultry products.

We have heard that the loss of markets is due to the shortage of dollars, but I have heard many farmers on Vancouver island ask whether it is a shortage of dollars or a shortage of foresight. They feel that no one man could possibly take such a broad view of things as would a group of men who were active producers.
